[White "1. Ruy Lopez: Rio de Janeiro - Intro & rare 6th moves"]
[Black "#03 6.d5 & 7.Ba4"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 { We are at the starting position of the Rio de Janeiro Variation of the Berlin Defense. The origin of the name is mysterious, since in databases you will find only one game in this line played in the Brazilian city more than 100 years after the variation's birth (Tal-Panno, 1987). In fact, it is one of the oldest variations known in chess and was upheld by such household names as Steinitz, Anderssen, Lasker, Tarrasch, Pilsburry, Reti, Smyslov, Spassky and others. However, in the 21th century, following the trend set by Kasparov-Kramnik 2000 World Championship match it has given way to l'Hermit Variation: 5...Nd6, leading to the famous Berlin Endgame (also called 'Berlin Wall') Thanks to an overwhelming popularity of 5....Nd6 nowadays, it is very probable that your fifth move: 5... Be7 will already come as a surprise to a majority of White players. If you are already a mainstream Berlin player, it can serve as a great alternative line. However, this line is not only an excellent surprise weapon. After analysing it thoroughly, I am confident that it is solid enough to be used as a main weapon even against high-level opposition. } 6. d5 { -KEY- } Nd6 7. Ba4 { This is only asking for trouble. } e4 { The best defence is offence! } 8. dxc6 exf3 9. cxd7+ Bxd7 10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. Qxf3 O-O-O $1 { Short castle is also fine but the text is more ambitious. } 12. Nc3 Rhe8 $15 { With the better development in a symmetrical structure, Black is the one playing for the win. } 
